WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.639
Idea. If you borrow ten dollars from a friend and they ask for eleven back, the extra dollar is interest. One dollar out of ten is ten per cent.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:21.207
An interest rate is the percentage charged for borrowing money, or paid as a reward for saving it. When rates change, businesses and their customers are both affected.

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:29.949
Method. Step one: fix the direction. Are interest rates rising or falling? Every effect depends on this.

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:39.074
Step two: split the effect into two channels. One is the business's own borrowing costs. The other is how much its customers spend.

00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:54.686
Step three: chain each channel to an outcome. When rates rise, repayments rise, so costs rise. Customers with loans have less to spend, and saving pays more, so demand falls. Either way, profit is likely to fall.

00:00:55.250 --> 00:01:06.319
Step four: use the case. Businesses selling expensive or non-essential items, such as cars, holidays or furniture, are usually hit hardest by higher rates.

00:01:07.450 --> 00:01:19.927
Worked example. Here is a real question from Paper two R in June 2024. Explain one impact on a business of an increase in interest rates. It is worth three marks.

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:26.877
Mark one is the impact. An increase in interest rates makes borrowing more expensive for the business.

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:36.163
Mark two explains why. If it has a loan or overdraft with a variable rate, it pays more interest each month, so its costs rise.

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:46.337
Mark three gives the consequence. Higher costs reduce profit, and the business may have to delay plans to expand, such as opening new shops.

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:57.796
Common mistake. Mistake one: getting the direction wrong. Higher interest rates do not increase sales. Customers have less to spend, so sales usually fall.

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:05.879
Mistake two: stopping at costs go up. Always finish the chain: what happens to profit or to the business's plans?

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:19.196
Your turn. Now you try. A furniture shop sells sofas, and many customers pay for them with a loan. Interest rates fall. Explain one impact on the shop. Pause and decide.

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:33.292
Answer. Rates are falling, so borrowing is cheaper for customers. Monthly repayments on a sofa loan fall, so more people can afford one. The shop sells more sofas, so its revenue rises.

00:02:33.850 --> 00:02:43.811
Remember the method: fix the direction, choose costs or customers, then link it to profit or plans. Now try the practice questions for this type.
